he command line works as it does in a window: the\nconfiguration file, media, ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"hXFcUWy3yi"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--whdload","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"wi46zyEEm1"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"kYooqvGo71"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--run","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"Wuc9BAQMjz"},{"type":"text","value":", the scheduled-input and\ncapture flags, ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"hBziT148MS"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--record-input","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"NblyuFzusp"},{"type":"text","value":", save states and warp boot.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"XrYvs5Qc14"}],"key":"qqTG1Aahfw"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal requirements","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"gjBDoSAkv1"}],"identifier":"terminal-requirements","label":"Terminal requirements","html_id":"terminal-requirements","implicit":true,"key":"Op8PwSNLDl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The terminal must speak the\n","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"hx07UV6x48"},{"type":"link","url":"https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/graphics-protocol/","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kitty graphics protocol","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"WG5D6aULmd"}],"urlSource":"https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/graphics-protocol/","key":"eTUKN24rz3"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the picture and, for full keyboard support, the\n","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"SzmIx87nTb"},{"type":"link","url":"https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/keyboard-protocol/","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kitty keyboard protocol","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"YGSwqcgPIP"}],"urlSource":"https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/keyboard-protocol/","key":"LnxuGcOmBq"},{"type":"text","value":".\nkitty, Ghostty and WezTerm support both. Copperline asks the terminal at start\nand stops with an error if it gets no graphics answer. tmux does not pass Kitty\ngraphics through, so run Copperline outside it.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"zYChZjI2VK"}],"key":"cGbWY7o6lZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The keyboard protocol matters because the Amiga keyboard reports every key\ngoing down ","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"wn3zafNmMR"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"and","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"A9FJlMfpsF"}],"key":"zhwOsDujXd"},{"type":"text","value":" coming up, and games read held keys. A terminal without the\nkeyboard protocol only says that a key was typed, so Copperline turns each key\ninto a short tap: typing works, but keys cannot be held, so keyboard joystick\nemulation and games that read held keys do not work. The status line says when\nthis is the case.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"sdWnyVgagS"}],"key":"YgzuA6dt5T"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Picture","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"jkmV3ChZdF"}],"identifier":"picture","label":"Picture","html_id":"picture","implicit":true,"key":"pkvbsuJlUX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frames are sent at the machine’s own resolution, compressed, and scaled by the\nterminal to the largest box that fits above the status line. Only frames that\nchanged are sent. When the connection cannot keep up, Copperline drops frames\ninstead of slowing the machine down, so emulation speed and sound are unaffected\nby a slow link. ","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"Ys2Vq7BvYS"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--terminal-fps N","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"lMghpiP6MN"},{"type":"text","value":" caps the frame rate to save bandwidth, for\nexample on a slow remote link:","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"tTlNip0nQz"}],"key":"leQAfvvEp6"},{"type":"code","lang":"sh","value":"copperline --terminal --terminal-fps 15 game.adf","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"jOkJ37VvNn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Workbench screen takes a few kilobytes per frame. A game that scrolls the\nwhole screen at 50 frames a second needs several megabytes a second; on a slower\nlink it is shown at a lower frame rate.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"kCHbVXvaCP"}],"key":"nddmzjpEy0"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keyboard, mouse and joysticks","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"J01klZu28c"}],"identifier":"keyboard-mouse-and-joysticks","label":"Keyboard, mouse and joysticks","html_id":"keyboard-mouse-and-joysticks","implicit":true,"key":"mgqPIKjHMC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keys reach the Amiga as in a window: the same keymap (F11 is Help, the Super\nkeys are the Amiga keys), and the same keyboard joystick bindings from\n","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"R8ZNbNHNb8"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[input]","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"e3KRFr23C3"},{"type":"text","value":". Copperline uses the physical key the terminal reports, so the\nlayout set on the Amiga decides which character a key types. Pasted text is\ntyped on the Amiga keyboard, as with Paste as Keystrokes in the window: the\ntyping starts about a third of a second after the paste, once the paste\nshortcut’s own modifier keys are up again, and a single paste is limited to\n64 KiB (the status line says when one was cut short).","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"Qj7uh8zZDo"}],"key":"lAOKXH19VA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a terminal without the keyboard protocol, Alt+","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"FBhwazsmXr"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"w2WN087B02"},{"type":"text","value":", Alt+","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"rGUOyPUAR1"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"O","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"cXNQmlsmwQ"},{"type":"text","value":", and Alt+","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Cp8s5XInP4"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Trq0Ios2HR"},{"type":"text","value":"\nsend the two bytes that also begin a control sequence. Copperline takes them\nfor the keys when nothing follows within a twentieth of a second.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"az28P07G4w"}],"key":"gA5LTegtZb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The terminal’s pointer moves the Amiga mouse by the distance it travels over the\npicture, as an uncaptured window does. The terminal cannot capture the pointer,\nand the guest applies its own mouse acceleration, so the two pointers drift\napart; pushing the pointer into a corner of the picture usually lines them up\nagain. Clicks count over the picture only. Gamepads connected to the machine Copperline runs\non work as usual.","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":67,"column":1}},"key":"qjxNmZIjyS"}],"key":"iVx6Ug16cH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the terminal loses focus, every key and button held on the Amiga is\nreleased, since the terminal will not report their releases.","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"key":"TUmaz8fgnM"}],"key":"mPyNH3UiJA"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host commands","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"Jc1KQkPyk7"}],"identifier":"host-commands","label":"Host commands","html_id":"host-commands","implicit":true,"key":"t96XAqsMwC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start"