position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":98,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"F12 then ","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"okV6z9YbrF"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"q","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"xq08y0SbnO"},{"type":"text","value":" is the way out from the keyboard. The terminal is in raw mode\nwhile Copperline owns it, so Ctrl+C and Ctrl+\\ are keys for the Amiga, not\ninterrupts.","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"uul2JoQZXT"}],"key":"cDwERetajg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":109,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A session also ends cleanly when the process is sent SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGINT\nor SIGQUIT -- by ","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"aGYPCvzv4W"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"JjtCKihUhD"},{"type":"text","value":", by ","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"pPsMe8fnWC"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeout","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"QIRkA4McRy"},{"type":"text","value":", or because the terminal window or SSH\nconnection closed. Copperline then leaves as it does for a quit and exits\nwith status 128 plus the signal number (143 for SIGTERM). That holds while\nthe session is still starting, too: a signal ends it even if the terminal’s\noutput is stopped (Ctrl+S, or a link that has stopped draining), and a\nterminal that takes no output for ten seconds at startup ends the session\nwith an error. It likewise ends, with an error, when the terminal stops\naccepting output or goes away, and when a capture flag has written its\ncapture.","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100